arXiv:2602.22710v1 [cs.SD] 26 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p">Broukhim
Weibel
Jolly</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Same Words, Different Judgments:
<br class="ltx_break">Modality Effects on Preference Alignment</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aaron
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nadir
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Eshin
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference-based reinforcement learning (PbRL) is the dominant framework for aligning AI systems to human preferences, but its application to speech remains underexplored. We present a controlled cross-modal study of human and synthetic preference annotations, comparing text and audio evaluations of identical semantic content across 100 prompts. Audio preferences prove as reliable as text, with inter-rater agreement reaching good levels (ICC(2,k) <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> .80) at <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>9 raters—the first ICC-based reliability characterization in the preference annotation literature for either modality. However, modality reshapes how people judge: audio raters exhibit narrower decision thresholds, reduced length bias, and more user-oriented evaluation criteria, with near-chance cross-modality agreement. Synthetic ratings further align with human judgments and predict inter-rater agreement, supporting their use both for triaging ambiguous pairs and as full replacements for human annotations.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>preference alignment, RLHF, audio language models, annotation reliability, cross-modal evaluation, text-to-speech, human preference data
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>
<sup id="id1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> University of California San Diego, La Jolla, CA 92093, USA
</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_email"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">email: </span>aabroukh@ucsd.edu, weibel@ucsd.edu, e3jolly@ucsd.edu</span></span></span><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
      <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
      
      
      
    Submitted for review to Interspeech 2026</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conversational audio models have seen rapid development and deployment in recent years. These systems aim to provide natural, responsive interactions aligned with user expectations, yet research on aligning audio models to human preferences remains sparse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Preference-based reinforcement learning (PbRL) offers a path forward, particularly for qualities like naturalness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, emotional expressiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and conversational appropriateness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, where vocal delivery may shape perception alongside semantic content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current preference alignment research centers on text, following a standard pipeline: collect pairwise human or AI preferences, train a reward model, and optimize via reinforcement learning algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. This approach has revealed biases such as annotators’ tendency to prefer longer responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In addition, text-based annotations introduce uncontrolled variables: attention allocation and reading order go untracked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Audio, by contrast, delivers information at a controlled and recordable pace, but cannot be presented side-by-side, so presentation order becomes an explicit variable. This affords researchers significantly more signals to consider during annotation with the caveat of potentially novel and more significant order effects. Despite these additional signals, much of the existing PbRL work for speech relies on Text-To-Speech (TTS)-generated audio with assumed labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we investigate the practical implications of data collection methods for PbRL. Specifically, we ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is audio preference data reliable?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does modality affect preference ratings?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can synthetic ratings substitute or augment human ratings?</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We find audio preference ratings to be as reliable as text preferences but behaviorally distinct, introducing biases such as recency effects, while showing reduced susceptibility to length effects. This paper is also the first to report Intraclass Correlation Coefficients (ICC) for preference labels in either modality, showing that a single annotator yields poor reliability, three yield moderate, and nine yield good agreement. Cross-modality agreement is near chance, with preference shifts varying by prompt rather than reflecting a uniform directional bias, suggesting that text data converted to audio via TTS may not be appropriate for audio preference pipelines. Lastly, synthetic ratings can be used to replace or triage ambiguous response pairs, reducing annotation costs without sacrificing label quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Preference-Based Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference-Based Reinforcement Learning is a framework in which a reward function is learned from human preferences rather than being explicitly specified. Given pairs (or sets) of trajectory segments or outputs, annotators indicate which they prefer, and these comparisons are used to train a reward model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. This reward model then serves as the optimization objective for a reinforcement learning agent. PbRL has been prominently applied to align Large Language Models with human values through Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), where preference data is collected by having humans or LLMs compare candidate responses to the same query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Standard PbRL pipelines typically rely on binary pairwise comparisons modeled with Bradley–Terry reward formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. While effective, binary labels discard information about preference strength and are susceptible to well-known order and position effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which are difficult to diagnose without additional modeling assumptions or counterbalancing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work suggests that richer scalar feedback can yield more informative reward signals than coarse comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and continuous rating paradigms such as PRISM’s visual analog scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> permit more expressive analyses of annotator agreement, including ICC and Krippendorff’s <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the practical trade-offs between binary and continuous annotation—including cognitive demands, calibration behavior, and implications for downstream reward modeling — remain inadequately characterized, particularly for audio settings where sequential presentation already complicates preference elicitation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Several known biases arise from this type of fine-tuning, including length bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, position bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and reward overoptimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Specifically, longer responses tend to win more frequently regardless of semantic content, and the first presented response is also more likely to be preferred.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In audio, less work has investigated the biases that may arise from annotating speech and how they relate to those found in text-based preference labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Many works convert text datasets to audio using TTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, implicitly assuming that data suitable for text is also suitable for speech. However, whether preferences and biases transfer across modalities remains an open question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Modality’s Effect on Judgments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing work on annotation biases in the speech literature has largely focused on quality assessment, cataloguing biases and proposing mitigations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Explicit cross-modality comparisons of preference judgments remain rare. Most similar to this work, Cho et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> demonstrated that preferences differ by modality and used this finding to build speech-adapted models, but their analysis did not extend to reliability scaling, order effects, decision thresholds, or synthetic-human alignment—dimensions we address here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More broadly, speech processing is strictly linear and imposes higher cognitive load than reading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and users tend to anthropomorphize audio interfaces more readily than text-based ones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, suggesting that evaluation criteria may shift across modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Reliability and Agreement in Preference Annotations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work on preference annotation reliability has generally reported only single-rater pairwise agreement—e.g., 73–77% for text summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, 63% for helpful and harmless annotations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and 60% for music <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. While intuitive, these percentages are not directly comparable across studies and, critically, do not address how agreement scales with the number of raters. Intraclass correlation coefficients and Krippendorff’s <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> offer richer characterizations by partitioning variance across raters and stimuli, and by accommodating different measurement scales, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. To our knowledge, no prior work has used these metrics to characterize the number of raters needed to reach specific reliability thresholds in preference annotation for either text or audio.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>The PRISM Dataset</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PRISM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is a preference dataset mapping the sociodemographics and stated preferences of 1,500 diverse participants from 75 countries to their contextual preferences in 8,011 live conversations with 21 LLMs. Participants rate each model response on a 1–100 visual analog scale (VAS) without visible numeric values to avoid anchoring. Conversations span three conditions—unguided, values-guided, and controversy-guided—to diversify prompts along the objective–subjective spectrum. Crucially, the person who initiates and steers each conversation is also the one who rates the responses, coupling the evaluative context with the interactive context. We use PRISM’s unguided conversations as our source material, adopting its VAS rating methodology and leveraging its original interactive ratings as a baseline for comparison with our external annotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We converted a subset of the PRISM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> text preference dataset to spoken audio using Kokoro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> for preference solicitation in the audio modality. In addition to the audio-to-audio judgments, we collected text-to-text comparisons matched in semantic content. To control for potential confounds introduced by audio fidelity, participants also evaluated the quality of each audio sample. This study was approved as minimal-risk research by the authors’ Institutional Review Board.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Converting Text to Audio</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sub-selected ”unguided” conversations to maintain a more objective dataset, other conversations were intended to be controversial. From the 3,113 unguided conversations, we randomly selected 100 interactions. To ensure variability in response lengths, 25% (25/100) of the interactions were drawn from the top 10th percentile of absolute character length differences between responses (i.e., a minimum difference of 449 characters). The rest (75/100) were drawn from the lower 90th percentile. This yielded 200 audio clips (2 model responses × 100 interactions). Audio clips were also screened so that only innocuous content was presented to participants, removing clips that could be construed as offensive, discriminatory, or emotionally distressing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each interaction was passed through the current state-of-the-art open-source text-to-speech model, kokoro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, as benchmarked by TTS-Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, to generate audio samples. We used kokoro’s default parameters, with the voice set to ’af_heart’ selected for its neutral, clear delivery and kept this consistent across all samples. All audio snippets were manually reviewed for artifacts or mispronunciations that could negatively impact quality to focus on semantic content. Low audio quality samples were replaced, and replacements were re-evaluated following the same selection criteria. We provide documentation of initial randomization, documented removals, followed by iterative replacements at the following <a href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1Dz9Hl7aaHf6TfscjhmIgRRj8kHFyFgNO2LK2PgGq2HY/edit?usp=sharing" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">link</a>. Removal reasons indicated by row color include: <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">yellow</span> = poor audio quality, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">orange</span> = other language, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">green</span> = empty output, and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">red</span> = harmful content. We also make publicly available all 3,113 audio converted unguided conversations at the following <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1OkRVfh7ltjBqiQUvUZ2HmLCHaihzHj6T?usp=drive_link" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">link</a> to encourage audio preference dataset collection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Participant Demographics by Condition</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
