</tbody>
<tfoot class="ltx_tfoot">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Values are <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD</annotation></semantics></math>) unless noted.</span></th></tr>
</tfoot>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Data Collection</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Demographic Task</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior to preference solicitation, participants completed a demographic questionnaire including: age, gender, race, ethnicity, birthplace, education level, employment status, country of residence, primary language, English proficiency (for non-native speakers), other spoken languages, and current timezone. Participants with no English proficiency and those under 18 were excluded. Participant demographics can be found in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Converting Text to Audio ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Preference Tasks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each interaction, a history of the conversation up to that point in text is shown to the user. The interface can be seen in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Preference Tasks ‣ 3.2 Data Collection ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Participants are then asked to rate the responses from 1 (Terrible) to 100 (Perfect) using a slider that doesn’t present the numerical value to avoid anchoring, as done in the original PRISM paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Each participant rates 20 interactions presented in a balanced block random order with an attention check at the beginning (21 total). The attention check paired a coherent response with a clearly nonsensical one; participants who preferred the nonsensical response were excluded.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22710v1/interface.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/368;" width="476" height="368" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Interface for the Audio Task of preference selection. It includes conversation history, most recent query and two responses. Raters must rate each response from 1-100 on response quality (the content of the answer) and audio quality (the clarity and naturalness of speech). We also collect discrete preferences with the option to tie and optional free-response reasoning justification.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Participants are randomly assigned to either <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">text</em> or <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">audio</em> response modality to prevent cross-condition contamination. Those in the audio condition also rate audio quality using the same 1-100 scale. Binary preference data, with the option to tie, are presented to respondents at the end. An optional text box asking raters why they preferred one response over the other is also presented to participants for qualitative analysis. We present responses sequentially since there is no way to present audio side-by-side, as can be done with text to minimize order effects, and aim to compare primacy or recency effects cross-modality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Audio responses must be played in full on the first listen—participants cannot skip ahead or scrub through the timeline. After the initial playback, participants can replay the audio as many times as needed, ensuring parity with the text condition where responses can be re-read at will.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to explicit ratings, we track behavioral and temporal indicators. We log: (1) total decision time (prompt onset to rating submission), (2) deliberation time in audio condition (time from completion of last audio listen to rating submission), (3) intermediate rating changes to capture preference reversals between responses during the evaluation process, (4) number of audio replays per response, (5) total session duration, and (6) completion rates across modalities to assess dropout patterns. For participants who change their ratings before final submission, we track both the number of reversals and, in the audio condition, whether reversals occurred after fully listening to both clips. We also track presentation and ordering effects by recording: which response appears first on screen and the sequential position (1-20) of each rating within a participant’s session to assess fatigue effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we record stimulus properties including: character count and number of conversational turns in the history for each interaction, response length (character count), and absolute character length difference between paired responses. Note, character length is linearly correlated with audio length, so we view these as interchangeable analysis signals. These metrics enable us to examine relationships between stimulus characteristics and inter-rater agreement patterns across modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Synthetic Ratings</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to human ratings, we collected synthetic ratings (10 per prompt per modality) to investigate alignment between synthetic and human signals, and to identify interactions where model responses are clearly differentiated versus ambiguous. We generate synthetic ratings (1–100) for text, audio, and audio-quality using GPT-4o for text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and GPT-4o-Audio-Preview for audio. We select GPT-4o-Audio-Preview since it is an end-to-end audio model and should present more insights than a speech-to-text (STT) model that uses an LLM and then converts responses back to speech. Such a model would only evaluate TTS and STT accuracy relative to LLM responses. We used default API parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These synthetic ratings serve as pre-computed AI scores that allow us to test relationships between real and synthetic interactions. Specifically, we’re interested in using synthetic signals to either augment human annotations or possible replace them as a whole for Reinforcement Learning from Artificial Intelligence Feedback (RLAIF) applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Participants and Exclusions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recruited 106 participants (53 per modality) from Prolific’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> US population, compensating them at a rate of $12 per hour. 7/53 (13.2%) participants in the audio condition and 5/53 (9.4%) in the text condition failed the attention check, leaving 46 and 48 participants, respectively. Of those who passed, 4 in the text condition did not complete the survey but we retain the trials they did complete, yielding a final sample of 94 participants. This resulted in each audio clip being evaluated by 8–10 participants. We break down the demographics of these 94 participants in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Converting Text to Audio ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Cross-Modal Rating Characteristics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision Threshold.</span> Raters exhibited larger average rating differences when committing to a winner (not a tie) when presented as text (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=41.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>41.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=41.7</annotation></semantics></math>, 95% CI <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[39.5,43.9]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>39.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>43.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[39.5,43.9]</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Mdn=34.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>34.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Mdn=34.0</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to audio (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=27.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=27.9</annotation></semantics></math>, 95% CI <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[26.1,29.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>26.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>29.8</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[26.1,29.8]</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Mdn=20.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>20.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Mdn=20.0</annotation></semantics></math>; Mann–Whitney <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=150582" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150582</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=150582</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{rb}=.303" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.303</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{rb}=.303</annotation></semantics></math>). Preference ties, while statistically different (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=34092" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>34092</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=34092</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.022" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.022</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.022</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{rb}=-.113" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.113</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{rb}=-.113</annotation></semantics></math>), were small in difference magnitude (Audio: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3.9</annotation></semantics></math>; Text: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3.3</annotation></semantics></math> on a 100-point scale). We represent this as a cumulative distribution function in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Cross-Modal Rating Characteristics ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following rating analyses were conducted using an omnibus linear mixed-effects model predicting raw ratings, with presentation order, standardized character count, standardized trial number, modality, and all modality interactions as fixed effects, and crossed random intercepts for participants and stimuli. Simple effects were probed via modality-specific models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22710v1/decision_threshold.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/340;" width="476" height="340"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Cumulative distribution of the mean per-prompt rating gap on trials where raters declared a preference (A or B rather than Tie).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">

