<p id="S4.T4.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">95% bootstrap CIs from stimulus resampling (2,000 for ICC; 5,000 for <math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>).
<math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values from permutation tests of the Audio–Text difference.</span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inter-Rater Reliability (Krippendorff’s <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
We computed Krippendorff’s <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> separately for continuous ratings, ordinal preferences (A/Tie/B recoded relative to presentation order; adjacent disagreements weighted less than distant), and nominal preferences (raw A/Tie/B labels without order correction; all disagreements weighted equally), with 95% CIs from 5,000 bootstrap resamples of stimuli. Unlike the ICC model, which partials out order effects via fixed-effect covariates, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> was computed directly on the raw rater<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>stimulus matrix without covariate adjustment. Modality differences were tested using the same permutation approach (5,000 iterations). No differences were statistically significant (all <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>; Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Reliability of Audio Preference Data ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Nominal <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values were near zero in both conditions, likely because counterbalanced presentation order caused raters who agreed on the preferred stimulus to assign opposite A/B labels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Modality Effects on Preferences</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-Modality Agreement.</span> To look at disagreement rates, we averaged raw ratings across participants in each modality for each response, calculated the winner, and compared winners across modalities. The good aggregate reliability (ICC(2,k) <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> .80) justifies the use of mean ratings. At a zero threshold (any nonzero difference counts as a win), cross-modality agreement was 53% (53/100 prompts; binomial test vs. 50% chance, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.31</annotation></semantics></math>), not reaching statistical significance. As the decisiveness threshold increased, agreement rose but number of decisive winners dropped, reflecting greater stability for strongly preferred pairs. When counting tie-decisive comparisons as disagreements, the agreement % drops until around a threshold of 10. We include Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Modality Effects on Preferences ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for a detailed view of agreement %’s relationship to agreement threshold.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22710v1/agreement_raw.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/137;" width="229" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Cross-modality agreement between audio and text evaluations as a function of raw rating gap threshold. The blue line shows agreement percentage when both modalities produce a decisive winner (excluding ties), while the red line shows agreement when tie-versus-decisive outcomes are counted as disagreements. Green bars indicate the number of prompts where both modalities had a decisive winner at each threshold.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt-Specific Modality Effects.</span> To test whether modality systematically shifted preferences, we computed per-trial preference scores (Clip<sub id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub">0</sub> <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> Clip<sub id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub">1</sub> ratings) and fit linear mixed-effects models with modality as a fixed effect and crossed random effects for prompt and participant. The fixed effect of modality was not significant (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.71</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.458" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.458</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.458</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating no overall preference shift between modalities. However, adding random slopes for modality by prompt significantly improved model fit (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(2)=22.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>22.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(2)=22.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>; random slope <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=12.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12.93</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=12.93</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that modality affected preferences differently across prompts—some prompts elicited stronger preferences in audio, others in text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justification Text Analysis.</span> To compare how participants explained their preferences across modalities, we analyzed 658 written justifications (347 audio, 311 text) using TF-IDF with Porter stemming (vocabulary = 223 terms). For each term, we tested whether document frequency differed by modality using <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> tests on presence/absence, with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=.05</annotation></semantics></math>). Excluding modality-specific terms, audio justifications more frequently mentioned “user” (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=12.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>12.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=12.8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) and “help” (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=9.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=9.8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.002</annotation></semantics></math>), while text justifications more frequently mentioned “response” (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=14.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>14.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=14.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), “detail” (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=14.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>14.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=14.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), and “give” (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=17.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>17.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=17.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting audio raters focused more on whether the response served the user’s needs while text raters attended more to the depth and specificity of the response content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>AI-Human Alignment</h3>

