<td id="S4.T5.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.2.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.2.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.374</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">Note.<span id="S4.T5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> For AI-Human error, <math id="S4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo mathsize="1.430em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents mean absolute error (MAE) between AI and human clip-level ratings on a 1–100 scale; the LMM row tests the modality difference. For PRISM comparisons, <math id="S4.T5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo mathsize="1.430em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the mean signed difference (PRISM <math id="S4.T5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> comparison group).
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average AI-Human Rating Error.</span> To test whether AI-human agreement differed by modality, we computed clip-level mean ratings for AI (synthetic) and human raters (raw ratings) separately for text and audio conditions, then calculated the absolute error <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|</annotation></semantics></math>AI <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> Human<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|</annotation></semantics></math> for each clip. A linear mixed model (LMM) with modality as a fixed effect and clip as a random intercept tested whether error differed significantly between modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PRISM vs. External Annotators.</span> To test whether the original PRISM ratings, where the person interacting with the model did the rating, differed from external annotators, we conducted paired <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests comparing PRISM ratings to each external group (AI-Text, AI-Audio, Human-Text, Human-Audio) at the clip level (raw score mean). We report our results in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4 AI-Human Alignment ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Notably, human-audio ratings aligned most closely with PRISM ratings (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=1.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=1.15</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.374" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.374</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.374</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that audio evaluation may better approximate the original interactive rating context, though the non-significant result precludes strong claims.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Difference Predicts Human Agreement.</span> To examine whether AI-generated rating discriminability predicts human inter-rater reliability, we computed per-prompt intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC(2,1)). For each prompt, we calculated the absolute mean difference between AI ratings for the two response options. We then fit a linear regression model predicting human ICC from AI difference, modality, and their interaction (ICC <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> AI_diff <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Modality). To assess robustness, we conducted Spearman correlations between AI differentiation and human ICC within each modality as a non-parametric check. We report the results in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.4 AI-Human Alignment ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. When AI ratings sharply distinguished between two responses, human raters also tended to agree more with each other, regardless of modality. Results were robust to removal of influential observations (Cook’s D).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Regression: AI Differentiation and Human Agreement</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="S4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Predictor</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">AI Differentiation</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.014</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>.001</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Modality (Text)</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.010</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.76</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">AI Diff <math id="S4.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Modality</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.003</td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">.15</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T6.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spearman correlations</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Audio</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" colspan="2"><math id="S4.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.23</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.02</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T6.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Text</th>
<td id="S4.T6.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" colspan="2"><math id="S4.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.38</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.T6.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">DV = per-prompt and modality ICC; <math id="S4.T6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study asked three questions about preference data collection for speech: <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> whether audio preferences are reliable, <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> how modality shapes preference judgments, and <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> whether synthetic ratings can supplement human annotation. Our results paint a consistent picture: audio and text preference data are comparably reliable in aggregate, <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">yet the two modalities produce meaningfully different evaluation dynamics</span>. Raters listening to audio operated with narrower decision thresholds, showed reduced sensitivity to response length, and described their preferences in terms oriented toward the user’s needs rather than the content’s depth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These differences did not manifest as a uniform directional shift—modality did not consistently favor one response over another—but instead interacted with specific prompts to reshape which response was preferred. In other words, modality changed <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> people judged, not whether they could judge reliably. Synthetic ratings complemented these human signals, identifying where annotators were likely to agree and offering viable proxy scores in both modalities. Together, these findings suggest that <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">audio preference annotation is a qualitatively different evaluative process</span>—one that may better approximate interactive text evaluation, and could thus serve as a cross-modal proxy for preference pipelines where collecting in-conversation ratings is impractical.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Audio Preference Reliability</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Singular Ratings Are Noisy; Aggregated Ratings Are Not.</span> We interpret ICC values in line with Koo et al.’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> conservative thresholds as shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Audio Preference Reliability ‣ 5 Discussion ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. At <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, agreement is poor (ICC(2,1) = .333 audio, .295 text), but aggregating to the observed <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}9</annotation></semantics></math> raters yields good agreement (ICC(2,<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span>) = .821, .788), with diminishing returns beyond that point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work on preference annotation reliability has generally reported only single-rater pairwise agreement—e.g., 73–77% for text summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, 63% agreement between annotators (helpful and harmless) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and 60% agreement between annotator and research team preferences (music) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>—but these percentages are not directly comparable to ICC and, critically, do not address how agreement scales with the number of raters. Alternative approaches hoping to collect one-off ratings rely on continuous ratings as a signal of decisiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, our work is the first to systematically characterize the number of raters needed to reach specific reliability thresholds in the preference annotation literature. Practically, collecting 3 ratings per stimulus moves agreement from poor to moderate at a 3<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> increase in annotation cost. Reaching good agreement requires approximately 9 raters—a 9<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> increase that may or may not be justified depending on the sensitivity and stakes of the downstream task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision Thresholds Are Smaller in Audio than Text.</span> Raters exhibited larger rating gaps between preferred and non-preferred responses in text (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=41.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>41.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=41.7</annotation></semantics></math>) than in audio (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=27.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=27.9</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{rb}=.303" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.303</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{rb}=.303</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>). This compressed margin matters for pipelines that convert continuous ratings into binary preference labels: a fixed binarization threshold calibrated on text data may misclassify audio pairs as ties or reverse preferences, inflating label noise for reward model training. We recommend calibrating thresholds per modality or, where possible, retaining the continuous signal to preserve preference strength information. We present Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Cross-Modal Rating Characteristics ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> as a resource for practitioners to select an appropriate decisiveness threshold given their tolerance for ambiguity, noting that the average rating gap when a binary preference was expressed was 41.7 (text) and 27.9 (audio) on a 1–100 scale.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22710v1/icc_by_k.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>ICC(2, <span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span>) as a function of the number of raters, derived from variance components of a crossed random-effects model. Shaded bands indicate conservative interpretation thresholds per <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. The observed average number of raters per stimulus was <math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\approx 9.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>9.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\approx 9.2</annotation></semantics></math> (audio) and <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\approx 8.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>8.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\approx 8.9</annotation></semantics></math> (text).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recency Bias Must Be Handled Carefully.</span> Audio preference labels present unique problems in how they are perceived relative to text. In text, preferences are often presented side-by-side to better account for order effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. For long audio clips, this presentation order becomes increasingly problematic since they can’t be visually compared like text can. Our results confirm a strong recency bias, with second-presented items rated significantly more favorably (<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=-2.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.61</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=-2.61</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.012</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, this effect did not differ by modality (<math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=-0.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.51</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=-0.51</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.732" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.732</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.732</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that recency bias is a general problem of sequential presentation rather than an audio-specific phenomenon. As employed in this study, counterbalanced presentation order across multiple raters can mitigate this bias at the aggregate level, though it inflates disagreement at the individual level—likely explaining why our nominal Krippendorff’s <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is close to 0. Re-listening to the first clip after hearing both has also been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and indeed roughly one quarter of audio trials in our study (214/920, 23.3%) involved participants voluntarily re-listening to the first clip after completing the second, suggesting a natural inclination to revisit earlier stimuli when making comparative judgments—though at the cost of increased session time. Binary ratings are especially susceptible to recency effects as there is no way to adjust for order effects post-hoc. We suggest using continuous ratings to better handle order effects when collecting audio preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Longer Rating Sessions Are More Efficient Without Loss of Data Quality.</span> Despite audio trials taking nearly twice as long as text trials (136 vs. 79 sec), participants rated both conditions as equally difficult (<math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.868" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.868</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.868</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that the additional time reflects the inherent pacing of audio rather than increased cognitive demand. Critically, ratings remained stable across trials in both modalities (<math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.960" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.960</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.960</annotation></semantics></math>) with no modality-specific drift (<math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.236" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.236</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.236</annotation></semantics></math>), even as completion times decreased significantly (audio: 8.6%; text: 16.0% per SD of trial number). This combination of practice-driven efficiency gains and rating stability implies that longer annotation sessions are feasible without sacrificing data quality, offering a practical path to reducing per-annotation costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">While time-on-task effects and cognitive fatigue are well documented in sustained attention and crowdsourcing contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, to our knowledge no prior work has examined fatigue or practice effects specifically for preference annotation in either modality. Our results suggest that sessions of approximately one hour are viable for both text and audio preference collection, though future work should identify the boundary at which rating quality begins to degrade.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Cross-Modality Considerations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text Preferences Do Not Generalize to Audio Preferences.</span> At a zero threshold, cross-modality agreement on the preferred response was only 53% (binomial test vs. 50%, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.31</annotation></semantics></math>), not reaching statistical significance. Agreement rose with increasingly decisive thresholds (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Modality Effects on Preferences ‣ 4 Results ‣ Same Words, Different Judgments:Modality Effects on Preference Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), suggesting that text and audio modalities converge, but only when preferences are strong. A mixed-effects analysis confirmed that this divergence is prompt-specific rather than reflecting a uniform directional shift: the fixed effect of modality was not significant (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.71</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.458" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.458</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.458</annotation></semantics></math>), but adding random slopes for modality by prompt significantly improved model fit (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}(2)=22.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>22.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(2)=22.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>; SD <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=12.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>12.93</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=12.93</annotation></semantics></math>). Given the near-zero fixed effect, this random slope magnitude implies that <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">modality could swing preferences by nearly half a standard deviation in either direction</span> depending on the prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our TF-IDF analysis of written justifications offers one interpretation consistent with this heterogeneity: audio raters more frequently referenced “user” and “help” (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.002</annotation></semantics></math>), while text raters emphasized “detail” and “response” (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math> for both). These lexical differences are compatible with audio evaluation promoting a more holistic, user-oriented frame—<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">does this response serve the person?</span>—while text evaluation encourages analytic attention to content depth and specificity. This would align with evidence that users anthropomorphize audio interfaces more readily than text-based ones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, potentially shifting evaluation from scrutinizing what was said to judging how well the response addressed the user’s needs. Prompts where these two evaluative frames diverge in which response they favor would naturally produce the modality-dependent preferences we observe.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audio Is Less Susceptible to Length Bias.</span> Length bias is a well-documented confound in text preference annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and our results replicate this: longer text responses predicted a roughly 40% larger positive rating increase compared to longer audio responses (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=4.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.92</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=4.92</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=3.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=3.52</annotation></semantics></math>; interaction <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.045</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.045</annotation></semantics></math>). The fixed pacing of audio likely constrains the salience of length as a heuristic—listeners cannot skim or visually gauge response size the way readers can. This reduced length sensitivity has practical implications: audio-based annotation may offer a more content-focused signal for training reward models, particularly in domains where length and quality are easily confounded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This extends the findings of Cho et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, who showed that excessive verbosity is penalized more heavily in audio than in text. Notably, Cho et al. also observed that their best-performing model generated longer responses than the base model while still being preferred, reinforcing that length bias in audio is moderated rather than reversed. Our per-stimulus estimates, drawn from approximately 9 raters at good ICC-based reliability, allow us to quantify this attenuation more precisely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One speculative but promising use case of this finding is using audio-derived labels as a complementary signal to text annotations—for example, upweighting audio-based reward scores to counteract length bias in text-trained reward models. This would come at the cost of longer annotation sessions (approximately 2<math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> per trial), and whether the debiasing benefit justifies the additional expense remains an open empirical question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Synthetic Rating Applications</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Ratings Identify Easy vs. Ambiguous Prompts.</span> When AI ratings distinguished between two responses, human raters also tended to agree more with each other (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.014</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), and this relationship held across both modalities with no significant interaction (<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.15</annotation></semantics></math>). Non-parametric checks confirmed robustness (audio: <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.23</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.02</annotation></semantics></math>; text: <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.38</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This finding has immediate practical value: <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">synthetic ratings can serve as a pre-screening signal to triage prompts before human annotation</span>. Prompts where AI models strongly differentiate between responses likely do not require large annotator pools, while prompts where AI ratings converge may warrant additional human raters to resolve genuine ambiguity. Such adaptive sampling could substantially reduce annotation costs without sacrificing label quality on the prompts that matter most. To our knowledge, this relationship between AI discriminability and human inter-rater reliability has not been previously reported, in part because ICC is rarely computed in the preference annotation literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Ratings Are Viable Proxies, With a Modality Caveat.</span> AI–human absolute error was significantly higher in the text condition than in the audio condition (<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=2.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=2.02</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.035" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.035</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.035</annotation></semantics></math>). While this margin is modest on a 100-point scale, it is notable given that audio raters operate with smaller decision thresholds (<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=27.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=27.9</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.7</annotation></semantics></math>)—a 2-point discrepancy represents a proportionally larger share of the signal that distinguishes preferred from non-preferred responses in audio.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work has reported approximately 80% agreement between GPT-4o and human judges for text-based evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, and RLAIF approaches have shown that AI-generated preferences can substitute for human labels in text domains with minimal performance loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Our results extend these findings to audio, suggesting that synthetic ratings are viable proxies in both modalities—though the tighter AI-human alignment in audio warrants further investigation into whether end-to-end audio models capture evaluative signals not available in text-based pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audio Annotation May Better Approximate Interactive Evaluation.</span> Among all external rating conditions, human-audio ratings aligned most closely with the original PRISM ratings, in which the person who initiated the conversation also rated the responses (<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=1.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=1.15</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.374" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.374</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.374</annotation></semantics></math>). By contrast, human-text (<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=8.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>8.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=8.03</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), AI-text (<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=-1.84" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.84</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=-1.84</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.015</annotation></semantics></math>), and AI-audio (<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{d}=-9.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9.18</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{d}=-9.18</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) ratings all diverged significantly. The non-significant result for human-audio precludes strong claims, but it is suggestive: the temporal pacing and sequential processing demands of audio may more closely approximate the evaluative context of a real-time conversational interaction in the text modality. If this pattern holds, it would have practical implications for preference data collection—audio-based annotation by external raters may serve as a closer proxy for actual user satisfaction than the text-based third-party annotation pipelines for <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">text-based interactions</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Implications for the PbRL Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, our findings suggest that the standard text-based preference collection pipeline may not straightforwardly extend to speech. The near-chance cross-modality agreement (53%) and prompt-dependent modality effects indicate that text preferences cannot be assumed to serve as reliable proxies for audio preferences—a critical consideration for systems that train reward models on text annotations and deploy them to score speech outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Practitioners designing PbRL pipelines for audio should account for several factors. <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span>, continuous ratings should be preferred over binary labels to enable post-hoc correction of the recency bias we observe in sequential presentation. <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span>, approximately 9 raters per stimulus are needed to achieve good aggregate reliability, though synthetic pre-screening can reduce this cost by identifying prompts where fewer annotators suffice. <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third</span>, audio annotation’s reduced susceptibility to length bias suggests that audio-derived reward signals may produce less biased reward models than their text counterparts, potentially justifying the roughly 2× increase in annotation time per trial. <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fourth</span>, the closer alignment between human-audio ratings and original interactive ratings raises the possibility that audio-based annotation pipelines may better approximate real user satisfaction than text-based alternatives—though this finding requires replication with audio-native data before informing production systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Converting text-based interactions to audio via TTS is currently the standard approach in the field for generating speech preference data, and our findings demonstrate that meaningful and reliable annotations can be collected from such stimuli. That said, TTS-rendered speech lacks paralinguistic nuances present in natural speech — such as emotional prosody, hesitation, and background noise — that may influence preference judgments in real-world audio interactions. Future work should investigate whether these findings replicate with audio-native datasets that capture these features, or with text prompts specifically written for spoken delivery rather than adapted from text conversations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, presenting conversation history and the user prompt in text for the audio condition likely introduces some noise; however, having users listen to full conversations in audio would be impractical. Retaining longer conversation histories also allows us to investigate whether the depth of prior context influences preference annotations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To strictly control for confounds, we utilized a single static voice profile (<span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">af_heart</span>). However, this limits our ability to generalize findings across different speaker identities. We do not assess how variable voice characteristics (e.g., gender, accent, pitch) might interact with semantic content to influence annotator preference or agreement rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our recruitment was limited to 106 participants from the US population. While this controlled for cultural variability in preference, it limits the generalizability of our findings. The patterns we observed may differ in non-Western populations or among non-native English speakers, who may process audio stimuli with different cognitive loads than native speakers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, our protocol actively removed interactions containing potentially harmful or offensive content. While necessary for participant well-being, this filtering excludes ”safety” and ”refusal” scenarios from our analysis. Consequently, we cannot determine if the audio modality amplifies or dampens human sensitivity to toxic content compared to text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study examined the reliability and characteristics of preference judgments across text and audio modalities. We find that audio preference annotations are reliable in aggregate and exhibit agreement levels comparable to text when sufficient raters are pooled. However, modality meaningfully shapes evaluation behavior. Preferences did not shift uniformly between text and audio, but instead varied by prompt, indicating that modality influences how responses are judged rather than which responses are preferred overall. Audio judgments showed narrower decision thresholds and reduced sensitivity to response length, suggesting that speech-based evaluation may attenuate known text biases such as length effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further show that synthetic ratings align with human judgments across modalities and predict human inter-rater reliability, highlighting their potential utility for adaptive annotation strategies. These results suggest that text-derived preferences cannot be assumed to generalize directly to speech outputs and that modality should be treated as a first-class consideration in preference-based reinforcement learning pipelines. Future work should validate these findings using audio-native datasets and more diverse speech conditions to better characterize how vocal and paralinguistic factors interact with human preference formation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As conversational AI systems increasingly operate across both text and speech modalities, establishing modality-appropriate annotation frameworks will be essential to ensuring that alignment research produces systems that genuinely serve users in the contexts they actually use them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Funding for this project was supported by the NIH National Library of Medicine’s T15 Biomedical Informatics and Data Science Research Training Program (Grant T15LM011271).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Generative AI Use</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative AI tools were used in the preparation of this manuscript for rephrasing and editing purposes.</p>
</div>
</section>
